{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom at afbb9dd80a52d0d1423ca782703be1874bfbecdf on request/commitment-honesty. Successor to 208dfcbd4deca48158ca6d10edd1e576e0cc484d.\n\nCloses codex's three findings on b0947a29.\n\nThe browser hid the state this change exists to reveal. revoked and released sat only in the closed set, while the fold and statusview deliberately keep both non-terminal, and the default board shows attention and hides closed — so an authority removing work that was not theirs vanished from the view a reader opens. Both are attention statuses now. That is the same defect I had just fixed in the Go lanes, reproduced one layer above them, which makes three layers on this branch: fold, statusview, browser. Not three mistakes but one habit of fixing the layer in front of me without asking what else expresses the same rule.\n\nThe fold-profile test now says what it does. Asserting the literal @3 pins this bump and fails if someone reverts it; it does not fail when a future change alters the fold's output and leaves the marker alone, which is the case a guard exists for and which I claimed it covered in five separate records — the test comment, the commit, the artifact, the report and the review request. Codex found it by reading the assertion instead of the sentence describing it. A real forward guard needs a projection fingerprint bound to the profile; that is not built here and nothing pretends otherwise now.\n\nThe work-loop page still said the fold projects eight statuses. My earlier edit to that line was a silent no-op — the replace pattern did not match, I asserted nothing, and I then reported the page as carrying ten. It does now, checked rather than assumed.\n\n\"released wants a new performer\" was wrong in the page and in the statusview comments. The request stays addressed to the same signed actor; what it can take is a replacement promise from that actor, unless the request itself is replaced. released is also explicitly not actionable, because treating a dead promise's row as active would double-count once a replacement exists.\n\nThe served bundle was rebuilt. The attention-lane change touched ui/src/lib/work.ts and the committed embed was still the previous build, so the resident would have served a board classifying both statuses the old way. This is the second time on this branch a UI source change shipped without its bundle, and the first was a blocker in review.\n\nThe lane-table mirror now carries a note about its own weakness: it catches a lane naming a status the fold cannot emit, and cannot catch the fold emitting a status no lane names.\n\ngo vet, the full Go suite, tsc, the browser tests and ui-check all pass, and the worktree is clean at this head.\n\nMerging must follow the resident-rebuild progress work, so a fold profile bump arrives with honest rebuild reporting.","body":{"branch":"request/commitment-honesty","commit":"afbb9dd80a52d0d1423ca782703be1874bfbecdf","head":"afbb9dd80a52d0d1423ca782703be1874bfbecdf","path":"internal/workroom"}}